  "taxTreatment": {
    "consequencesStated": [
      {
        "text": "Historically, pensions have not formed part of your estate for IHT purposes, making them a valuable estate planning tool. However, legislation has now been passed to change this.",
        "topic": "IHTEstatePlanningChange",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "linkedObjectiveIds": [
          "OBJ7"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            8
          ],
          "quote": "pensions have not formed part of your estate for IHT purposes, making them a valuable estate planning tool. However, legislation has now been passed to change this"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT. Exceptions include pensions paid to a surviving spouse or civil partner, dependents\u0027 scheme pensions, and death-in-service benefits.",
        "topic": "IHTChangeApril2027",
        "isClientSpecific": false,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            8
          ],
          "quote": "From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "You can draw benefits from your pension fund in a number of different ways from age 55 (rising to 57 from 6 April 2028). However, you should always take advice beforehand to ensure you are taking benefits in the most tax efficient way possible.",
        "topic": "BenefitDrawnageTaxPlanning",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            15
          ],
          "quote": "You can draw benefits from your pension fund in a number of different ways from age 55 (rising to 57 from 6 April 2028). However, you should always take advice beforehand to ensure you are taking benefits in the most tax efficient way possible"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "Should you die before age 75, the nominated beneficiaries can receive a lump sum, drawdown an income or purchase a dependents annuity. All these options are paid free of tax. Where you die after the age of 75, the same options are available, but tax is paid at the beneficiary\u0027s marginal rate of tax.",
        "topic": "BeneficiaryTaxTreatmentByAge",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            15
          ],
          "quote": "Should you die before age 75, the nominated beneficiaries can receive a lump sum, drawdown an income or purchase a dependents annuity. All these options are paid free of tax. Where you die after the age of 75, the same options are available, but tax is paid at the beneficiary\u0027s marginal rate of tax"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "Personal contributions to the pension benefit from valuable tax relief; for example, a net contribution of \u00A380 effectively becomes \u00A3100 gross in your pension.",
        "topic": "TaxReliefOnContributions",
        "isClientSpecific": false,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            15
          ],
          "quote": "Personal contributions to the pension benefit from valuable tax relief; for example, a net contribution of \u00A380 effectively becomes \u00A3100 gross in your pension"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "You can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of \u00A3268,275 for people with no form of lifetime allowance protection). The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment from which the individual can take regular or ad-hoc income withdrawals to suit their needs and circumstances. There is no upper limit to the amount the individual can withdraw as an income. However, each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax.",
        "topic": "FlexiAccessDrawdownTaxation",
        "isClientSpecific": false,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            28
          ],
          "quote": "You can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of \u00A3268,275). The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment. Each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "Flexi-access drawdown also provides a tax-efficient way of passing wealth down through the generations as any unused pension fund can usually be passed down to a wide range of beneficiaries free from Inheritance Tax.",
        "topic": "PensionInheritanceTaxEfficiency",
        "isClientSpecific": false,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            28
          ],
          "quote": "any unused pension fund can usually be passed down to a wide range of beneficiaries free from Inheritance Tax"
        }
      }
    ],
